window.docContents[33].resolve({"/What-a-p-adic-L-function-should-be/Special-values-and-arithmetic/#An-introduction-to-p-adic-L-functions-___-Lean-blueprint--What-a-p-adic-L-function-should-be--Special-values-and-arithmetic":{"contents":"There are deep results and conjectures relating special values of -functions\nto arithmetic invariants. A prototype is the class number formula.\n\nLet  be a number field with  real embeddings,  pairs of complex\nembeddings,  roots of unity, discriminant , regulator  and class number\n. Then  has a simple pole at  with residue\n\nThis rests on . (Mathlib formalises this as the\nstatement that  tends to the displayed residue as\n along the reals.)\n\nThis is a classical analytic class number formula; we record it for motivation\nrather than prove it. The left-hand side is an analytic invariant of the\nmeromorphic function , while the right-hand side is a product of\narithmetic invariants of . The bridge between them comes from comparing the\nDirichlet series  with a sum over lattice points in the ideal class\ngroups: the residue at  counts ideals of bounded norm, which by the geometry\nof numbers is governed by the covolume of the unit lattice (the regulator ),\nthe number of roots of unity , the discriminant , and the number of ideal\nclasses .\n\n(Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture.) Let  be an elliptic curve. Then the\nMordell–Weil group  is finitely generated, and\n\nMoreover the leading Taylor coefficient of  at  is given by an\nexplicit product of arithmetic invariants of . This concerns\n.\n\nThis is an open conjecture, included to motivate the -adic theory; no proof is\nclaimed. As with the class number formula, the left-hand side is analytic and the\nright-hand side arithmetic, and the two worlds are so different that even the\nexistence of the left-hand side — that  is defined at  — requires\nthe analytic continuation supplied (over ) by the modularity theorem. The\nknown low-rank cases proceed through Heegner points and Euler systems, which give\nthe two inequalities  and\n, respectively, when the relevant Tate–Shafarevich group \nis finite. These same Iwasawa-theoretic tools are what the present notes develop\nin the simpler setting of the Riemann zeta function.\n\nIwasawa theory seeks -adic analogues of such statements, replacing complex\nanalysis (poorly suited to arithmetic) by -adic analysis (where arithmetic\narises naturally). For an elliptic curve  and each prime  there is an\nIwasawa Main Conjecture equating a -adic analytic -function with\n-adic arithmetic invariants of ; the bottom row of the BSD/IMC square is\nfar more tractable than the top, and the IMC for elliptic curves is known much\nmore widely (Kato; Skinner–Urban) than BSD itself. In these notes we treat the\nsimplest instance of the picture: the Main Conjecture for the -adic Riemann\nzeta function, formulated by Iwasawa, which is known completely for every prime.\n\n","context":"An introduction to p-adic L-functions — Lean blueprint\u0009What a p-adic L-function should be","header":"2.2. Special values and arithmetic","id":"/What-a-p-adic-L-function-should-be/Special-values-and-arithmetic/#An-introduction-to-p-adic-L-functions-___-Lean-blueprint--What-a-p-adic-L-function-should-be--Special-values-and-arithmetic"}});